=== The groups $U$ of types $F_4$, $twisted(2, F_4)$ and $twisted(2, E_6)$
<sec:l2012-extremal-en-f4-e6>

For the root system $Phi$ of type $F_4$, we need notation from
[@bib:l2012-extremal-en-Levchuk1990].

By [@bib:l2012-extremal-en-Bourbaki1968, Tables I–IV] and
[@bib:l2012-extremal-en-Levchuk1990], the positive roots of systems of types
$A_(n-1)$, $B_n$, $C_n$, $B C_n$, and $D_n$ may be written as

$ epsilon_i-m epsilon_j = p_(i,m j), quad 1 <= j <= i <= n, m = 0,1,-1. $

Set $T_(i v) = T(p_(i v))$ for exception the case
$T_(i 1) = T(p_(i,-1)) T(p_(i 1))$ for type $D_n$. If $U G(K)$ is a group of
classical type distinct from $A_n$ then, by [@bib:l2012-extremal-en-Levchuk1990,
Lemma @lem:l1990-chevalley-classical-root-centralizers (II)], the centralizer
$C(T_(i v))$ in $U G(K)$ coincides with $T_(1,-v-1)$, when either $i < n$ or
$G = twisted(2, A_m)$ or $2K = K$, $G = C_n$; in the remaining cases, we have
$C(T_(n v)) = T_(1,-v-1) T_(n,n-1)$.

Let $C_n^+ = {p_(i v) | 0 < |v| <= i <= n, v != i}$, as above. For type $B_n$,
we set $epsilon_i-m epsilon_j = q_(i,m j)$. By analogy with
[@bib:l2012-extremal-en-Levchuk1990], we represent the positive system $F_4^+$
as the union $C_4^+ union B_4^+$ with the given intersection

$
  B_4^+ inter C_4^+ = {q_(i 0),p_(i,-i) (1 <= i <= 4)}, quad
  B_4^+ = {q_(i j) | 0 <= |j| < i <= 4}.
$

Also, we use the following diagram from [@bib:l2012-extremal-en-Levchuk1990;
@pass:l1990-small-f4-root-representation[root diagram]]. (The roots are
accompanied by the notation $(a b c d)$ from
[@bib:l2012-extremal-en-Bourbaki1968, Table VIII].) The substitution
$overline(" "): Phi arrow.r Phi$ is defined by the simple rule:
$overline(p_(i j)) = q_(i j)$, $overline(q_(i j)) = p_(i j)$
($1 <= |j| < i <= 4$).

